# Objective

Closes #14888.

## Solution

Add non-consuming trigger functions:

```rust
impl World {
    pub fn trigger_ref(&mut self, event: &mut impl Event);
    pub fn trigger_targets_ref(&mut self, event: &mut impl Event, targets: impl TriggerTargets);
}
```

## Testing

- Added two new tests, `observer_trigger_ref` and `observer_trigger_targets_ref`.

pr_comments:
I have a concrete use-case for this in my networking library. When a client attempts to connect to a server in my crate, we trigger a `SessionRequest` event that contains connection info like address and headers. The consumer of the crate must then make a decision based on those headers on whether the client should be allowed to connect or not.

Without the ability to read back the triggered event, consumers must trigger a 2nd separate event `SessionResponse` to determine whether the client can connect or not. If they don't send this event, then the client never connects, and I would have to add more explicit handling to say, "if we don't get a SessionResponse by the end of this frame, disconnect them and log a warning".

I use a oneshot channel to forward the response to an async task. If I can't immediately send a value along this channel, then I have to keep this channel around in a component on the client's entity. This isn't a deal-breaker, but just harms ergonomics when there is a possible cleaner solution to my problem.

Current code:

```rs
// when we get a new connection request...
        let session = commands
            .spawn((
                ClientFrontend::Connecting {
                    // here's the oneshot channel over which the SessionResponse is sent
                    send_session_response: Some(connecting.send_session_response),
                    // ...
                },
            ))
            .set_parent(server)
            .id();
        let _ = connecting.send_session_entity.send(session);

        let request = SessionRequest {
            headers: connecting.headers, // ...
        };
        commands.trigger_targets(request, session);

// when the user responds to this request...
fn on_connection_response(
    trigger: Trigger<SessionResponse>,
    mut clients: Query<&mut ClientFrontend>,
) {
    let client = trigger.entity();
    let Ok(mut frontend) = clients.get_mut(client) else {
        return;
    };
    let ClientFrontend::Connecting {
        send_session_response,
        ..
    } = frontend.as_mut()
    else {
        return;
    };
    let Some(sender) = send_session_response.take() else {
        return;
    };

    let _ = sender.send(*trigger.event());
}
```

Ideally:

```rs

// when we get a new connection request...
        let session = commands
            .spawn(ClientFrontend::Connecting { .. })
            .set_parent(server)
            .id();
        let _ = connecting.send_session_entity.send(session);

        let mut request = SessionRequest {
            headers: connecting.headers, // ...
            response: SessionResponse::Undecided,
        };
        commands.add(move |world: &mut World| {
            world.trigger_targets_ref(&mut request, session);
            connecting.send_session_response.send(request.response);
        });
```

This also means that I can immediately validate whether the consumer of my crate actually set the SessionResponse, without having to wait until the next frame:
```rs
world.trigger_targets_ref(&mut request, session);

if request.response == SessionResponse::Undecided {
    warn!("User code did not provide a response to connecting client {session}");
}

connecting.send_session_response.send(request.response);
```

I expect that this approach of, "I'm sending out this event to ask if X can do Y", can be applied in a lot more situations as well.

> I'm a bit weary of giving Events in observers special treatment when compared to Events in EventWriters.
> Why is it not enough to let the observers change a component that you then query later?
> If there is a reason, why does this not apply to EventWriter::send as well?

IMO the fact that you have mutable world access and are immediately applying changes to the world marks a big difference between EventWriters and Triggers. Changing a component can be unergonomic (see my concrete use case above), and this pattern of "send an event -> allow consumers to change the outcome of the event -> read back the event" is only possible when you have this mutable world access. It's also an established pattern in other software e.g. [the Minecraft server software Paper](https://papermc.io/), where while listening to an event, you can choose to change certain parameters, or even cancel it entirely.

issue_context.body:
## What problem does this solve or what need does it fill?

I want to perform cleanup after `Observer`s have been triggered with an event, given they are passed into observer systems as `&mut Event`, i.e.:

```rust
let mut foo: FooEvent = FooEvent { /* ... */ };
world.trigger_ref_mut(&mut foo);
// then do something with the modified `FooEvent`.
```

## What solution would you like?

```rust
impl World {
    pub fn trigger_ref_mut(&mut self, event: &mut impl Event);
    pub fn trigger_ref_mut(&mut self, event: &mut impl Event, targets: impl TriggerTargets);
}
```

## What alternative(s) have you considered?

Getting commands ordered in specific ways for applying.

## Additional context

I believe this ability is required for a possible "deferred egui" model that I'm ruminating on.
